Generic single-database configuration.

Before deploying a schema change, read these.

Migrations run themselves on startup
------------------------------------
`init_db()` (app/core/db.py) runs `alembic upgrade head` when the application
boots, serialised behind a Postgres advisory lock so concurrent replicas do
not race the same DDL (#296). Two consequences worth holding on to:

  * A migration that fails is a backend that does not start.
  * A migration is applied at deploy time, not at a moment someone chose to
    watch. Anything destructive runs unattended.

That is why this project requires every autogenerated migration to be read
and hand-trimmed before it is committed, and why `--autogenerate` output has
repeatedly contained operations belonging to nothing anyone was working on.
See #217, and the docstring of
versions/506252dfc555_add_tool_install_run_216.py for a worked example.

Revisions with an operational cost
----------------------------------
Some revisions need a decision from whoever is deploying, not just a review.
Where that is the case it is written in the revision's own docstring. Read it
before rolling out:

  * b1d4f7a09c62_add_finding_scan_query_indexes_217.py -- builds five indexes
    on `finding` and `scan`. On a large deployment run them by hand ahead of
    the deploy (the statements are in the docstring, and the revision is a
    no-op once they exist). Otherwise every other replica blocks on the
    migration lock through the whole build and its readiness probe kills it.

Deferred schema changes
-----------------------
Drift between models.py and the deployed schema that is known and
deliberately not yet applied, each needing a data audit against a real
deployment first, is tracked in #437 (NOT NULL flips, enum conversions, a
constraint swap) and #438 (the orphaned `discoveredendpoint` table). Both
carry the audit queries. Do not apply any of it from autogenerate output
without running those first.
